    "summary": "High-energy Pigpen-led set with scorching Bertha and stellar China>Rider",
    "blurb": "Features a blistering Bertha opener and a tight China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider sequence, with Pigpen steering the second set through classics like Good Lovin' and El Paso. The band delivers high‑energy playing throughout, captured in crystal‑clear soundboard recordings.",
    "review": "The March 27, 1972 show at New York's Academy of Music finds the Dead riding a wave of pure, Pigpen‑driven momentum. From the opening burst of \"Bertha\"—Jerry’s twang‑laden guitar and Ron’s harmonica pushing the tempo—to the seamless segue of \"China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider,\" the first set never loses its groove. Phil’s backing vocals shine on the China/Rider pair, while Bob offers a tender take on \"Looks Like Rain.\" \n\nThe second set keeps the energy high without resorting to marathon jams; highlights include a rousing \"Good Lovin'\" that lets Bill's crisp drumming cut through the mix, a rollicking \"El Paso,\" and the soulful \"Greatest Story Ever Told\" where Donna Jean’s Southern‑roots harmonies add extra color. Across both sets the band feels tight, inspired, and fully in sync—especially with Keith’s piano punctuations and Ron’s occasional organ swells. All of this is captured on an excellent soundboard source that delivers a balanced, noise‑free listening experience.",
